  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n(Saturday - Sunday)\n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Isaias have shifted slightly east across \nthe Tennessee Valley this weekend. Consequently, the chances for a \nwidespread, soaking rainfall have dropped across our southeast \nMissouri and southwest Illinois counties. The exact track of the low \nlooks to come down to internal dynamics, with individual vorticity \nmaxima rotating cyclonically around the broader midlevel \ncirculation. Predicting specific internal dynamics are near \nimpossible 24+ hours out, so there still may be a wobble \nlongitudinally. A farther west (east) track would increase \n(decrease) the chances of light rain. As alluded to above, the \nlatest track from the National Hurricane Center is slightly east. \nProbabilities for 0.25+\" and 0.50+\" have dropped significantly \non the latest LREF, now generally below 10-20%. In fact, \nchances for as low as 0.10\" have fallen as low as 15-30% even \nalong the southeastern CWA border. While some light rain still \nseems likely in southeast Missouri and southwest Illinois, a \nvast majority of the area will stay dry. There will be at least \nsome thickening mid/high level clouds moving northward on \nSaturday. These clouds should \"cool\" temperatures this weekend \nback into the low to mid 80s for most locations. \n\n\n(Sunday Night - Monday Night)\n\nAfter a brief lull in the near-record warmth due to cloud cover \nassociated with the remnants of Hurricane Isaias, it looks to return \non Monday. The air mass still appears to be more impressive compared \nto Friday, with 500-hPa heights from the NAEFS above the 95th \npercentile of climatology and 850-hPa temperatures close to +20C\n(near record for the date). Surface winds also should turn more\nout of the southwest, which is more favorable for downslope \nwinds off of the Ozark Plateau. Daily record high temperatures \ncertainly look to be in jeopardy, with even the monthly record \nin St. Louis (94F on 10/11/1963) potentially under threat. Low \ntemperatures are also expected to be on the upswing due to \nincreasing surface winds and more cloud cover (especially Monday\nnight). Readings are forecast to only drop back into the 60s \neach night for most locations.\n\n\n(Tuesday - Next Thursday)\n\nForecast spread increases for mid week, mainly due to uncertainty \nwith the evolution of a cold front. This boundary is likely to move \nthrough at least parts of the area on Tuesday before wavering back \nnorth as a warm front later in the week. Confidence in temperatures \nis quite low due to the wavering front and uncertainty in its \nexact location. The inter-quartile range from the NBM is around \n15 degrees each day, helping illustrate the high spread in high \ntemperatures. Chances of showers (and some thunderstorms) also \nincreases during this period as low-level moisture convergence \nstrengthens along with multiple midlevel impulses moving along \nthe crest of the ridge axis.",
